**Key findings:**

1. **Multi-signal advantage holds across classifier families.** LR-3 vs LR-1: +$65.48 (p = 2.2e-40, d_z = 6.03); RF-3 vs RF-1: +$75.98 (p = 3.2e-37, d_z = 5.17). The signal-driven gain is not NCC-specific.
2. **NCC outperforms LR on the same signals** (cam_multisignal +$206.13 vs lr_multisignal +$184.44, −$21.69, p = 3.6e-24) because NNC is Bayes-optimal for the Gaussian generative model; LR's linear boundaries underfit the cluster structure.
3. **RF slightly beats NCC** (rf_multisignal +$209.79 vs cam_multisignal +$206.13, +$3.66, p = 1.0e-12, d_z = 1.35) — RF's nonlinear boundaries learn the exact cluster edges from the 2000-sample calibration set, achieving 100% match. This confirms the 3-signal structure is fully learnable and the NCC ceiling (98.9%) is a classifier-family limitation, not a signal limitation.
4. **RF-1 outperforms LR-1 and NCC-1** (rf_intent_only +$133.81 vs cam_learned +$155.38 vs lr_intent_only +$118.96) — RF's nonlinearity partially compensates for missing signals, but the multi-signal gain (RF-3 vs RF-1: +$75.98) still dominates.

**Interpretation:** The multi-signal classification gain is robust across three classifier families (NCC, LR, RF). The gain magnitude varies by family (NCC: +$103.85 hand-set / +$50.01 learned; LR: +$65.48; RF: +$75.98), but the direction is consistent: 3 signals > 1 signal. RF-3's 100% match confirms the clusters are well-separated; the residual headroom above NCC (98.9%) is classifier capacity, not signal structure. **Resolved (Section 8.12):** Offline-trained epsilon-greedy (epsilon=0.1, +$169.91) and LinUCB-style linear profit model (+$91.42) both underperform cam_multisignal (+$206.13); the reward landscape is nonlinear.

### 8.9 Macro-F1 and Common Random Numbers

**Macro-F1 (§9.2 #11):** Match rate (accuracy) is unbalanced under the skewed class distribution (exploration 35%, consideration 30%). Macro-F1 averages per-class F1, penalizing classifiers that never predict minority classes. Results (seed 1, 200 scenarios):

- `rf_multisignal`: 1.0000 (perfect — RF learns the exact cluster boundaries)
- `lr_multisignal`: 0.9312
- `cam_multisignal`: 0.9254
- `cam_multisignal_learned`: 0.9194
- `rf_intent_only`: 0.7279
- `cam_learned`: 0.7061
- `ncc_intent_only`: 0.5342
- `cam_inferred`: 0.4696 (emits only 4 of 6 classes — never CRISIS or RETENTION)
- `lr_intent_only`: 0.4632

Macro-F1 confirms the multi-signal advantage at the per-class level: 3-signal classifiers (0.92–1.00) substantially outperform 1-signal classifiers (0.46–0.73). `cam_inferred`'s low macro-F1 (0.4696) reflects its structural handicap (4-of-6 classes), which match rate (75.7%) overstates.

**Common Random Numbers (§9.2 #10):** The benchmark seeds a single global `random` stream per seed, so agent *k* sees the stream after agents 1…k−1 have consumed it — the *context* is paired but the *decision noise* is not. CRN (per-agent RNG, same contexts) changes profits by <$3 for all stochastic agents (baseline: +$0.81; noisy50: −$2.62; noisy80: +$0.86). All deterministic agents (cam_multisignal, cam_learned, etc.) are unaffected. CRN does not change any conclusion; the paired t-test remains valid (§9.2 #10).

### 8.10 Channel-Dependent Reward Condition

The channel-cost arbitrage (Section 8.7) arises because BASE_REWARDS are indexed by (action_type, situation), not channel: a context-blind EMAIL policy (EDUCATIONAL|EMAIL|bid 0.3) earns +$252.41, beating every deployable context-aware agent. We test a channel-dependent reward condition (`channel_mismatch_penalty = 0.5`): when the agent's channel differs from SITUATION_CHANNEL[situation], total reward is multiplied by 0.5. Results (50 seeds x 200 scenarios): cb_edu_email_030 drops from +$252.41 to +$132.72 (-47.5%), while cam_multisignal is nearly unchanged (+$206.13 -> +$205.77, -0.2%). The arbitrage disappears: cam_multisignal now beats cb_edu_email_030 by $73.05. situation_only (+$294.27) and oracle (+$210.00) are unaffected (they always use the correct channel). This confirms the arbitrage is a reward-design artifact, not a feature of context-aware decisioning.

### 8.11 Adversarial-Mapping Condition

To test whether the benchmark can falsify a wrong mapping (Section 9.2 #1), we construct an agent with perfect 3-signal classification (98.9% situational match) but a deranged action mapping (EXPLORATION->PROMOTIONAL, CONSIDERATION->CRISIS_RESPONSE, DECISION->EDUCATIONAL, CRISIS->LOYALTY, OPPORTUNITY->COMPARISON, RETENTION->URGENT). Results: 0.4% match against the true IDEAL_ACTION, profit -$268.88 (worse than the random baseline, -$170.03). The contrast vs cam_multisignal is +$475.01 (p = 5.23e-96, d = 29.28). Perfect classification with a wrong mapping is catastrophic, not merely suboptimal: the mapping itself carries the value, and the benchmark is falsifiable via profit.

### 8.12 Contextual-Bandit Baselines

Two offline-trained bandit baselines (Section 10.3 #12): (a) epsilon-greedy (classify situation via 3-signal NCC, epsilon=0.1 random action, else Q-argmax = ideal action) and (b) a LinUCB-style linear profit model (ridge regression per action type on (1, intent, comp, quality) -> profit, trained on 2000 calibration contexts). Results: epsilon-greedy earns +$169.91 (90.1% match, exploration tax -$36.22 vs cam_multisignal, p = 2.95e-31, d = -2.25); LinUCB earns +$91.42 (75.6% match, -$114.71, p = 4.13e-48, d = -7.35). Both underperform cam_multisignal (+$206.13). The linear profit model's poor performance confirms the reward landscape is nonlinear: a ridge regression on 3 signals cannot capture the situation->action mapping that the NCC classifier exploits.

### 8.13 Class-Conditional Label Noise

We replace uniform epsilon-corruption with class-conditional noise (Section 9.2 #16, Section 10.3 #13): crisis<->decision and retention<->exploration are the documented confusable pairs. At 10%/20%/30% corruption rate (only these 4 of 6 classes are corrupted; consideration and opportunity keep clean labels), cam_multisignal_learned's match drops from 98.7% to 96.3%/93.0%/89.2% and profit from +$205.39 to +$198.20/+$186.59/+$172.27. The 30% class-conditional contrast is -$33.11 (p = 1.13e-34, d = -2.13). Class-conditional noise is less damaging than uniform noise at the same rate (uniform 30% retention noise drops match to 84.9%, Section 8.5.2) because only 4 of 6 classes are corrupted.

### 8.14 Convergence Trend Test

We test for slow monotone drift (Section 9.2 #17) with Spearman rho of profit vs seed index (1..50) and a Geweke z-score (first 20% vs last 50%). cam_multisignal: rho = -0.301 (p = 0.034, marginally significant), Geweke z = 2.04 (borderline). baseline: rho = 0.189 (p = 0.188), z = -1.05. cam_learned: rho = -0.176 (p = 0.221), z = 0.39. The marginal cam_multisignal trend (rho = -0.301) reflects slightly higher profits in early seeds, not a systematic drift; the Geweke z = 2.04 is borderline (|z| < 1.96 would be non-significant). The paired t-tests (which use per-seed differencing) are robust to this trend. Conclusion: no strong convergence concern, but the marginal trend is reported for transparency.

### 8.15 Perception-Only Dose-Response

To de-confound perception from bidding (Section 9.2 #4, Section 10.3 #4), we run NoisyCAM with flat bidding (bid = 1.0, no situation-based multiplier) at p_correct in {0.5, 0.6, 0.7, 0.8, 0.9, 1.0}. Results: profit increases monotonically from +$128.79 (p=0.5, 58.1% match) to +$294.27 (p=1.0, 100% match). Per-seed Spearman rho(profit, p) = 0.998 (min 0.943). This confirms the perception->profit dose-response is not an artifact of bid policy: even with flat bidding, better perception yields higher profit. The p=1.0 profit (+$294.27) equals situation_only (+$294.27), confirming that flat bidding is optimal when perception is perfect; the oracle's 2.0x crisis multiplier is suboptimal (Section 8.16).

### 8.16 Crisis Bid Policy Alternatives

The default crisis response is CRISIS_RESPONSE with a 2.0x bid multiplier (Section 6.5). We test three alternatives (Section 9.2 #23, Section 10.3 #17): pause (bid = 0.0), normal (bid = 1.0, no multiplier), and counter (2.0x, current default). Results (50 seeds x 200 scenarios): in the default environment, pause earns +$225.10, normal +$215.62, counter +$206.13. In the crisis_heavy environment (25% crisis situations), the gap widens: pause +$243.79, normal +$192.87, counter +$141.93. Pausing (bid = 0) is consistently better than counter-messaging (2.0x): the 2.0x crisis multiplier overbids in low-reward crisis situations. This finding does not invalidate the action mapping (CRISIS->CRISIS_RESPONSE is still correct) but suggests the bid multiplier should be calibrated, not hardcoded.

### 8.17 Sensing-Layer Fidelity

The `quality_score` signal is a decoy (uniform 0.5-1.0, uncorrelated with the situation-linked `channel_quality`); classifiers read `context.channel_quality` directly (Section 9.2 #24, Section 10.3 #18). We test whether replacing the decoy with the real `channel_quality` (and adding `competitive_density` as a signal) changes results. With `sensing_fidelity = True`, all four classifier agents produce identical profits: cam_multisignal +$206.13, cam_multisignal_learned +$205.39, cam_learned +$155.38, cam_inferred +$104.26. This confirms the sensing layer is decorative: classifiers already use the real context scalars, not the signals list. Multi-modal sensing claims should be understood as testing the classifier on the three real signals (intent, competitive density, channel quality), not the sensing infrastructure.

### 8.18 Online Reward-Feedback Learning (v1.4)

The §8.12 bandit baselines were **offline-trained** — the Agent ABC has no reward-feedback loop (§10.3 #12, §9.2 #13). Yet §8.11 showed the mapping itself carries the value. We therefore ask: **can an agent learn the situation→action mapping from observed profit alone, within a single 200-step episode?** Two online agents mirror `cam_multisignal` exactly — same 3-signal classifier, same channel rule (`SITUATION_CHANNEL[sit_est]`), same heuristic bid — and differ *only* in the action decision, which comes from a learned policy instead of the fixed `IDEAL_ACTION` table:

- `online_eps_q010` — ε-greedy (ε = 0.1) over (estimated situation × action type) Q-cells with optimistic init (Q₀ = 2.5), least-tried tie-breaking, incremental-mean updates from observed profit
- `online_ucb` — UCB1 over the same cells (exploration bonus c = 0.5)

Q-cells reset each episode (no cross-seed learning); the runner calls the agent's `observe(context, action, result)` hook after each evaluation. Deterministic: stdlib random seeded per seed, fixed agent order (`results/cam_sim_v14.json`).

| Agent | Match % (default) | Profit (default) | Profit (crisis_heavy) |
|---|---|---|---|
| baseline | 21.6 | −$170.03 | −$206.50 |
| cam_multisignal (fixed mapping) | 98.9 | **+$206.13** | **+$141.93** |
| online_eps_q010 | 76.6 | +$110.76 | +$46.15 |
| online_ucb | 83.8 | +$142.13 | +$76.76 |

**Findings.** (1) **Reward feedback alone recovers most of the mapping value:** UCB1 closes 83.0% of the baseline→`cam_multisignal` profit gap in the default environment (81.3% in crisis_heavy); ε-greedy closes 74.6% / 72.5%. Both remain significantly below the fixed mapping (UCB1: −$64.00, p = 2.8e-59, d = −4.13; ε-greedy: −$95.37, p = 3.7e-50, d = −5.76). (2) **The label-free online learner beats the hand-tuned classifier:** `online_ucb` (+$142.13) outperforms `cam_inferred` (+$104.26) without a single labeled action example (paired +$37.87, p = 4.8e-25, d = 2.37; ε-greedy also wins: +$6.50, p = 4.7e-03), though it stays significantly below `cam_learned` (+$155.38, 2,000 labeled samples; paired −$13.25, p = 7.6e-10). (The online runner reproduces the core benchmark byte-identically for the shared deterministic agents — baseline −$170.03, `cam_inferred` +$104.26, `cam_multisignal` +$206.13 — validating pairing fidelity.) (3) **The bottleneck is rare situations:** crisis and opportunity have 5% base rates (≈10 episode visits each), so their Q-cells stay under-sampled within a 200-step window — the learned mapping converges on high-frequency situations first. (4) **Scope:** channel and bid rules are given (only the action mapping is learned); joint (action × channel × bid) learning is confounded by the §8.7 channel-cost arbitrage and left open, as is online adaptation of the bid layer.

**F10 (online recovery bound):** the mapping's value is *partially* recoverable from pure reward feedback (72.5–83.0% of the fixed-mapping gap), but labeled situation data remains the efficient path to full value — supervised classifiers trained on 2,000 labels dominate online learners at 200 steps.

### 8.19 Mid-Episode Distribution Drift (v1.4)

The F5/F6 distribution-shift experiments run **full episodes** under shifted presets (§8.4). Real deployments face drift *within* the deployment window. We build a drift sequence: **scenarios 1–100 drawn from the default distribution, scenarios 101–200 from crisis_heavy** (crisis base rate 5% → 25%; economics identical — crisis_heavy differs from default only in situation weights). Every agent sees the identical sequence; "recalibrated" agents are fit on 2,000 labeled samples from the post-drift (crisis_heavy) distribution — the same labeled-sample assumption as F6.

| Agent | Match % | Profit | H1 (default) | H2 (crisis_heavy) |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| baseline | 18.4 | −$186.02 | −$84.54 | −$101.48 |
| situation_only | 100.0 | +$303.75 | +$147.53 | +$156.22 |
| cam_inferred | 63.5 | +$17.56 | +$51.66 | **−$34.09** |
| noisy50 | 59.0 | +$24.18 | +$26.46 | −$2.28 |
| cam_learned_def (default-fit) | 75.5 | +$74.68 | +$77.98 | −$3.30 |
| cam_learned_drift (post-drift fit) | 73.8 | +$59.59 | +$47.70 | +$11.89 |
| cam_multisignal (static) | 98.9 | **+$174.07** | +$102.82 | +$71.25 |
| cam_ms_drift (post-drift fit) | 98.7 | +$173.44 | +$102.27 | +$71.17 |
| oracle | 100.0 | +$178.03 | +$104.82 | +$73.21 |

**Findings.** (1) **F5 replicates within-episode:** the hand-tuned classifier's systematic bias collapses in the shifted half (+$51.66 → −$34.09, vs noisy50's −$2.28), and the default-fit learned classifier also goes negative in H2 (−$3.30). (2) **The F6 remedy reverses under mid-episode drift:** post-drift recalibration fixes the shifted half (+$11.89 vs −$3.30) but *costs more* in the un-shifted first half (+$47.70 vs +$77.98) — **net −$15.08 vs the default-fit classifier (p = 1.56e-05, d = −0.71)**. Recalibration to a detected shift presupposes the shift persists for the rest of the deployment window; refitting the whole episode to the post-shift distribution trades first-half performance for second-half recovery. (3) **Multi-signal inference is drift-robust:** `cam_multisignal` stays strongly positive in both halves (+$102.82 / +$71.25) and per-distribution refitting is unnecessary (`cam_ms_drift` +$173.44 ≈ static +$174.07). (4) Remedies consistent with §8.18: drift detection + windowed or online refits — not whole-window refits to the latest distribution.

### 8.20 Flat-Taxonomy Confusion Quantification (v1.4)

Limitation #18 argues the flat 6-way taxonomy conflates three constructs (funnel stage, market condition, lifecycle state) and *forces* confusions an orthogonal design would not have. We quantify that cost: the intent-only interval classifier (fit on 2,000 default samples, standard protocol) and the hand-set 3-signal NCC classifier are applied to 12,000 fresh default-distribution samples; for each (true, predicted) cell we additionally measure the expected profit of playing the *predicted* situation's full policy (ideal action + channel + heuristic bid) under the *true* situation (300 contexts per cell).

**Confusion rates.** The intent-only classifier concentrates its errors exactly in the cross-axis pairs #18 predicts: crisis→decis
