### 8.21 LLM Zero-Shot Classification Baseline (v1.5)

The pre-registered protocol of §10.3 #24, executed on 2026-11-02 (`paper/cam_sim_v15_llm.py`; results `results/cam_sim_v15.json`, response cache `results/llm_cache/`). Each context is textualized into a natural-language observation — intent strength, competitive density, channel quality, and the verbatim signals list *including the decoy `quality_score`* — and sent to an LLM at temperature 0 whose system prompt states the six situations as **qualitative marketing definitions only** (no numeric centroids, no examples; the full prompt is in the repo for audit). The returned label drives the same fixed pipeline as every CAM agent: `IDEAL_ACTION` action type, `SITUATION_CHANNEL` channel, oracle-style bid. The agent is therefore *identical to `cam_multisignal` except for the classifier* — any profit difference is attributable to classification quality alone. Evaluation runs on the identical per-seed context sequences (pairing-guard asserted against `run_online_env` byte-identically).

**Protocol deviation (documented):** the pre-registered 50-seed protocol (10,000 calls per model) exceeded measured provider quotas (SAIA: 800 requests/day; Groq: daily token caps; measured 2026-11-02), so per-model seed counts are reduced and reported exactly: `zai/glm-4.7` 10 seeds (2,000 calls), `saia/qwen3.6-35b-a3b` 2 seeds (400 calls), `groq/qwen3.8-27b` 3 of 5 planned seeds complete (600 calls; provider token quota), `tud/mistral-medium-3.5-128b` 10 seeds (2,000 calls; TU Darmstadt llm-service), `local/deepseek-v4-flash` 5 seeds (1,000 calls; LAN-local reasoning model — the only quota-free deployment, ~9 s/call). All comparisons remain paired on identical sequences; scenario-level contrasts (n = seeds × 200) are primary, seed-level t-tests secondary (low power at n < 10).

