Seed-level contrasts for glm-4.7 (n = 10): vs `cam_inferred` +$14.55 (p = 6.8e-05), vs `cam_learned` −$36.13 (p = 2.0e-06), vs `cam_multisignal` −$91.60 (p = 8.8e-09), vs oracle −$95.61 (p = 1.9e-09). TUD Mistral-128B (n = 10 seeds): vs `cam_inferred` +$17.62 (p = 1.4e-04), vs `cam_learned` −$33.06 (p = 1.3e-08). Local DeepSeek (n = 5): vs `cam_inferred` −$20.99 (p = 8.2e-03), vs `cam_multisignal` −$126.17 (p = 3.7e-05). All five models fall **significantly below** `cam_learned` and `cam_multisignal` at scenario level (the dense 27B ties `cam_inferred`).

**Findings.** (1) **LLM zero-shot classifiers are profit-competitive but balanced-classification-inferior:** raw accuracy (74–83%) is inflated by class imbalance — a no-skill classifier sampling the funnel trio (exploration 36.5% + consideration 30.4% + decision 13.4% = 80.3% of contexts) by base rate scores 80.3% — and all five macro-recalls (50.0–61.3%) sit **below** the hand-tuned `cam_inferred`-style classifier's 65.9% (recalls 100/100/98/5/45/46). The profit advantage over the handicapped single-signal classifier is real but comes from *within-trio* discrimination (big-3 recall 87–100%) plus prior collapse, not from better balanced classification. The lead evidence is the pair that survives the 610-way Bonferroni correction (threshold p < 8.2e-05): glm-4.7 **+$0.07** per scenario (p = 8.9e-06) and Mistral-128B **+$0.09** per scenario (p = 1.2e-08); qwen3.6-35b's +$0.10 (p = 8.1e-03; does not survive 610-way Bonferroni correction) and qwen3.8-27b's −$0.01 (p = 0.70, tie) are not significant after correction, and DeepSeek's −$0.10 per scenario (p = 4.9e-06) is a significant negative result. All five lose decisively to the 2,000-label supervised classifiers (−$33 to −$126 per seed; all p < 1e-04) and to oracle-with-heuristic-bid. **Prompting is not a substitute for labels in this environment.** (2) **The LLMs' blind spots are emergent, structural, and identical across model families:** no model *ever* predicts crisis (glm: 0/94 correct — 83% misread as decision; Mistral-128B: 0/94, 68% as decision; DeepSeek: 0/36, 100% as decision) or retention (glm: 0/195 — 73% as exploration; Mistral-128B: 0/195 — 100% as exploration; DeepSeek: 0/105 — 88% as exploration); qwen3.6-35b: crisis 0/14, retention 0/40; qwen3.8-27b: crisis 0/19, retention 0/61. Mistral-128B is the sharpest collapse: 100% correct on exploration/consideration/decision but it *never leaves those three* — opportunity is misread as consideration 88% of the time, exactly §8.20's costliest cross-axis pair after crisis→decision. All five therefore emit at most 4 of 6 classes — the *same* structural handicap that §9.2 #5 attributes to the hand-threshold `cam_inferred`, here arising from the models' own priors with no threshold rule in sight. (3) **The blind spots concentrate on the classes whose scalar signatures overlap adjacent classes** (crisis intent 0.8 vs decision 0.9; retention intent 0.3 vs exploration 0.2) and exactly on the *costliest* confusion (crisis→decision, −$2.79, §8.20) — while the discriminative rescue sits in the second/third signal's *centroid values* (crisis: channel_quality 0.20 vs ≥ 0.55; retention: competitive_density 0.15 vs ≥ 0.45), which the fitted multi-signal classifier exploits for 98.9%. Zero-shot semantic competence cannot replace the centroid calibration that makes multi-signal inference work. (4) **Cost/practicality:** qwen3.6-35b classified at 0.12 s/call (≈ 274 prompt tokens/call), Mistral-128B at 0.49 s (533.6k prompt + 6.0k completion tokens for 2,000 calls), glm-4.7 at 5.2 s (thinking model); all at $0 API cost (academic/free tiers). The LAN-local DeepSeek-V4-Flash (9.0 s/call, reasoning in a separate field; 340k prompt + 159k completion tokens for 1,000 calls) needs no provider at all — a deployable on-premise agent reaches 74.1% with commodity local hardware. The decoy `quality_score` was included verbatim in every observation; no model's errors concentrated on it. **seed-1 sensitivity (dev-on-test check, computed from the response cache):** dropping seed 1 shifts accuracy only slightly — glm-4.7 79.9→79.6 (−0.3pp), Mistral-128B 80.3→79.7 (−0.7pp), qwen3.6-35b 83.0→79.5 (−3.5pp, only 2 seeds), qwen3.8-27b 75.7→75.0 (−0.7pp), DeepSeek 74.1→73.2 (−0.9pp) — i.e. no pilot overfitting (the largest delta belongs to the 2-seed model).

**F11 (LLM zero-shot ceiling):** LLMs at temperature 0 — from a LAN-local reasoning model to a 128B dense model — classify CAM-Sim contexts at 74–83% raw accuracy, but that accuracy is prior-inflated: **macro-recall is only 50–61%** (below the hand-tuned classifier's 65.9%) with zero recall on crisis and retention; skill concentrates in the funnel trio (big-3 recall 87–100%), which is where 80.3% of contexts live. qwen3.6-35b's nominal +$0.10 vs `cam_inferred` (p = 8.1e-03; does not survive 610-way Bonferroni correction) is not a reliable win — the surviving contrasts are glm-4.7 (+$0.07, p = 8.9e-06) and Mistral-128B (+$0.09, p = 1.2e-08), with DeepSeek significantly negative (−$0.10, p = 4.9e-06). LLM situation classification is thus at best a *label-free starting point* — profitable at the margin but balanced-classification-inferior — while supervised multi-signal classifiers remain the efficient path to full value in this environment. The v1.6 arms LOCALIZE the zero-recall blindness as **prior-driven** (0/210 counterfactual probes ever elicit crisis/retention) and show that prompt remediation restores recall only at a profit/accuracy cost (hints: Mistral +$7.49, glm -$57.75; few-shot: both -$69 to -$88), while end-to-end action selection (v1.6 arm H) exceeds cam_multisignal in both families (+$273.64 / +$250.28).

**Scope:** classification-only per the pre-registration (the action/channel/bid mapping is given, isolating classifier quality); prompt-level remediation (few-shot, qualitative hints, self-consistency) and end-to-end LLM action selection are now tested empirically in v1.6 (section 8.22); fine-tuned LLM classifiers remain future work. Responses are cached (one snapshot; providers do not guarantee bitwise determinism at temperature 0 — exact regeneration requires fresh API calls). Provider rate limits (documented above) make this the benchmark's first experiment whose sample size is externally constrained; the protocol scales to the full 50 seeds as quotas allow.

### 8.22 Prompt-Variant Diagnostics, Remediation, and End-to-End Agency (v1.6)

This v1.6 extension localizes the 8.21 blindness cause and tests remediation vs end-to-end agency. It is a strict pre-registration addition (item 24 follow-up), all numbers from `results/cam_sim_v16.md`, no fitted components, seed-level bootstrap CIs (10k reps, RNG 20261103). Eight arms: A order-permutation, B minimal-labels, C counterfactual-archetype probes, D decoy-removal, E qualitative-hints, F few-shot, G self-consistency, H end-to-end action selection. Arms A, B, C, D, G are descriptive diagnostics (no confirmatory claim); arms E, F, H carry inferential contrasts (profit means and model-vs-reference CIs).

**(A) Diagnostics — order, minimal-labels, decoy, self-consistency, counterfactual probes.** Arm A (Mistral, seed 1): six fixed permutations of the six class-definition blocks yield accuracy 86.5 / 86.5 / 86.5 / 86.5 / 84.0 / 86.5 — order-insensitive (max spread 2.5pp, same emission pattern). Arm B (minimal-labels, seed 1): Mistral 86.5% with definitions = 86.5% without — definitions contribute nothing to Mistral; glm-4.7 drops 83.0% (with definitions) → 74.0% (bare) — definitions matter for glm only. Arm D (decoy-removal, Mistral, seed 1): removing the decoy quality_score yields 86.5% with vs 86.5% without, delta exactly 0.0pp — decoy fully ignored. Arm G (self-consistency, seeds 1-5, crisis+retention contexts only, k=5 at temperature 0.7, majority vote): both families crisis recall 0/36 and retention recall 0/105, identical to zero-shot — sampling cannot rescue a prior collapse. Arm C (counterfactual archetypes, all five 8.21 models, 42 probes each = 210): synthetic observations at the six canonical centroids plus +/-0.15 scalar nudges. NO model EVER emits crisis or retention across all 210 probes, INCLUDING the exact crisis centroid (intent 0.8, competitive 0.85, quality 0.2 → all five say decision/consideration) and the exact retention centroid (0.3/0.15/0.8 → all say exploration). Blind spots are **prior-driven** emission collapse, not in-distribution discriminability failure: no scalar configuration induces the labels.

**(B) Remediation — hints (E) and few-shot (F).** Arm E (qualitative-hints, seeds 1-5; v15 zero-shot prompt + two QUALITATIVE hint sentences, no numbers): Mistral zero-shot: acc 81.7 (seeds 1-5), macro 50.0, crisis recall 0, retention recall 0, profit +$137.15; Mistral + hints: acc 83.8, macro 60.1, crisis recall 61.1, retention recall 0, profit +$144.64 [128.39, 161.13]; vs cam_inferred +$29.53 [10.43, 49.50]; vs cam_multisignal -$75.65 [-92.76, -57.66]. glm zero-shot: acc 79.6 (seeds 1-5), macro 61.1, crisis 0, retention 0, profit +$127.35; glm + hints: acc 61.3, macro 68.1, crisis recall 86.1, retention recall 27.6, profit +$69.60 [63.99, 76.94]; vs cam_inferred -$45.51 [-57.41, -33.47]; vs cam_multisignal -$150.69. Mechanism of 8.21 finding (3) confirmed causally — verbalizing the discriminative signal value lifts crisis recall from 0% (both families; glm to 86%, Mistral to 61%) and glm retention to 27.6%, WITHOUT numeric centroids. BUT the profit effect is family-divergent: +$7.49 to Mistral, -$57.75 to glm (glm's hint-augmented responses over-call off-funnel classes, trading profit for balanced recall). Mistral retention stays blind (0%): its separator (competitive_density 0.15 vs exploration ~0.5) resists qualitative verbalization on that family.

Arm F (few-shot, seeds 1-5; six compact hand-written exemplars, one per class, rounded values): Mistral few-shot: acc 68.0, macro 54.1, crisis recall 69.4, retention recall 3.8, profit +$49.42 [30.22, 64.88]; vs cam_inferred -$65.69; vs cam_multisignal -$170.87. glm few-shot: acc 62.1, macro 65.6, crisis recall 75.0, retention recall 61.9, profit +$58.19 [47.16, 69.23]; vs cam_inferred -$56.91 [-72.04, -41.63]; vs cam_multisignal -$162.10. Few-shot exemplars teach off-funnel signatures (Mistral crisis 0→69%; glm retention 0→62%) but distort the emission prior toward uniformity, collapsing accuracy (-12 to -17pp) and profit (-$69 to -$88) in both families. Prompting remediation is a trade-off, not a free win.

**(C) End-to-end (H) headline.** Arm H (end-to-end, seeds 1-5; LLM outputs the full decision line 'situation; action; channel; bid' from menus: six situations × six actions × five channels × five bid tiers {0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 1.0, 1.4}, plus six normative sentences; evaluated directly via evaluate_action on the canonical per-seed env; invalid/failed → fixed neutral fallback Action(educational, search, 1.0) counted as invalid): Mistral end-to-end: profit +$273.64 [260.67, 286.61], ideal-action match 81.6%, invalid 0/1000; vs cam_inferred +$158.53 [142.71, 174.76], vs cam_multisignal +$53.35 [39.63, 67.21], vs oracle +$49.15 [35.19, 63.76]. glm end-to-end: profit +$250.28 [240.90, 259.57], match 74.7%, invalid 13/1000; vs cam_inferred +$135.18, vs cam_multisignal +$29.99 [19.31, 40.94], vs oracle +$25.79 [14.65, 37.38]. **Headline:** end-to-end semantic agency beats the ENTIRE fixed-mapper stack in both families, including cam_multisignal (supervised on 2,000 labels) and the situation-only oracle row (fixed bids) — with NO fitted component. The LLM natively chooses pause/loyalty/urgent actions for off-funnel situations, reproducing the crisis-policy insight (§8.16: pause beats counter) from normative text alone. Proper wall-clock ceiling remains bid_calibrated +$530.45. First agent class to exceed the fixed-mapper ceiling in either family without any fitted component.

**(D) Synthesis.** The 8.21 blindness is prior-driven and structural (invariant to order/definitions/decoy/sampling, universally non-elicitable at any centroid) but prompt-addressable at a cost (E/F trade balanced recall against profit), while end-to-end agency (H) escapes to above the fixed-mapper ceiling with no fitted component.

