## 10. Conclusion & Future Work

### 10.1 Summary
We presented **CAM-Sim** — a reproducible paired-seed benchmark for situational-awareness ablations in marketing agents. In 50 seeds × 200 scenarios (10,000 evaluations per agent), multi-signal situation classification (intent + competitive density + channel quality) raises match from 87.5% (learned single-signal) to 98.7% (learned multi-signal) and profit from +$155 to +$205 (+$50, p = 8.5e-32, d_z = 3.96); against a hand-tuned threshold classifier, the gain is +$102 (p = 5.4e-46). A matched-family ablation (same NCC family, 1 vs 3 signals) isolates the gain as signal-driven (+$103.85 (hand-set 3-signal vs fitted 1-signal), p = 5.16e-46; +$103.10 (fitted 3-signal vs fitted 1-signal), p = 1.60e-45); a majority-action floor (35.1%, −$15.38) retires the random-action baseline (strongest on the SEARCH channel). A context-blind policy search reveals a **channel-cost arbitrage**: EDUCATIONAL on EMAIL (cost $0.05) at bid 0.3 earns +$252, beating every deployable context-aware agent in 7 of 9 environments (§8.7) — the environment's reward is channel-independent, so the cheapest channel dominates; the multi-signal gain is measured *within* the context-aware ladder, not against all context-free policies. The advantage replicates across 9 environments (per-seed ρ ≥ 0.93), survives 30% label noise and budget pacing, and is checked by paired-difference bootstrap (B = 2000, CIs within $0.30), macro-F1 (per-class), and common random numbers (CRN changes profits by <$3). Standard classifier baselines (LR, RF) on 1 and 3 signals confirm the multi-signal gain is classifier-family-robust (LR +$65.48, RF +$75.98; RF-3 achieves 100% match). Three v1.4 extensions close further gaps: online reward-feedback learners recover up to 83.0% of the fixed-mapping value without labeled action data (§8.18); under mid-episode distribution drift, recalibrating to the post-shift distribution is net-negative (−$15.08, p = 1.6e-05) while multi-signal inference is drift-robust (§8.19); and the flat taxonomy's forced confusions are quantified at −$1.63 to −$2.79 per confused scenario, concentrated exactly in the predicted cross-axis pairs (§8.20). The v1.5 extension executes the pre-registered LLM baseline: zero-shot classifiers reach 74–83% raw accuracy — profit-competitive with the hand-tuned classifier but below it on balanced accuracy (macro-recall 50–61% vs 65.9%), all losing to supervised classifiers, with emergent ≤4-of-6 blind spots on the overlap classes (§8.21, F11); the v1.6 extension shows the blindness is prior-driven (0/210 counterfactual probes ever elicit crisis/retention), prompt remediation restores recall only at a profit/accuracy cost (hints: Mistral +$7.49, glm -$57.75; few-shot: both -$69 to -$88), and end-to-end LLM action selection exceeds cam_multisignal without any fitted component (+$250–273 vs +$206–220). All profits are scaled-reward proxies (§9.2 #12). The central practical finding: invest first in **multi-signal situation classification**, then in **mechanism-calibrated bidding** — but channel-cost structure can dominate signal quality (§8.7); unvalidated bid heuristics are worse than not bidding at all.

### 10.2 Contributions
1. **Benchmark (Empirical):** CAM-Sim — a reproducible paired-seed benchmark with an 11-agent situational-awareness ladder and 9 environment presets; profit spans −$170.03 (random-action baseline) to −$15.38 (majority-action floor) to +$252.41 (context-blind EMAIL policy, §8.7) to +$294.27 (perfect action matching) to +$530.45 (situation knowledge + mechanism-calibrated bidding); monotone environment-consistency in perception quality (per-seed ρ ≥ 0.93 lower-CI in all 9 environments)
2. **Findings (Empirical):** Four novel findings — (F3/F8) miscalibrated context-inflated bidding is *worse than flat bidding* (replicates 8/9 environments, survives budget pacing); (F5/F6) systematic classifier bias under distribution shift beats coin-flip adversely, and per-distribution recalibration fixes it robustly to 30% label noise; (F6) match rate is not profit — error *placement* modulates the profit relationship; (F9) multi-signal awareness (intent + competitive density + channel quality) raises match from 87.5% (learned) to 98.7% (learned) and profit by +$50 (p = 8.5e-32, d_z = 3.96); against a hand-tuned classifier, +$102 (p = 5.4e-46). **Standard classifiers (LR, RF) confirm the gain is classifier-family-robust (§8.8):** LR +$65.48 (p = 2.2e-40), RF +$75.98 (p = 3.2e-37); RF-3 achieves 100% match, slightly beating NCC (+$3.66). Note: F9's universality is established on the handicapped comparator; the learned-vs-learned per-environment comparison is deferred (§10.3 #11).
3. **Ablation (§8.6, §8.7):** Matched-family ablation (same NCC family, 1 vs 3 signals) isolates the multi-signal gain as signal-driven, not classifier-capacity-driven (+$103.85 (hand-set 3-signal vs fitted 1-signal), p = 5.16e-46, d_z = 7.89; +$103.10 (fitted 3-signal vs fitted 1-signal), p = 1.60e-45; d_z is t/√50 from a deterministic paired comparison — it measures simulation reproducibility, not a generalizable effect size); majority-action floor (35.1%, −$15.38) retires the random-action baseline (strongest on the SEARCH channel); **context-blind policy search (§8.7)** reveals a channel-cost arbitrage — EDUCATIONAL|EMAIL|bid 0.3 earns +$252.41, beating every deployable context-aware agent in 7 of 9 environments — the paper claims multi-signal value *within* the context-aware ladder, not dominance over all context-free policies; paired-difference bootstrap (B = 2000) confirms normal-approximation CIs within $0.30.
4. **Methodological:** Bootstrap BCa CIs (200 resamples) provide a non-parametric check on normal-approximation CIs (§9.2.2; caveat: B = 200 is underpowered, §9.2 #15); paired-difference bootstrap (B = 2000, §8.6) confirms the normal-approximation CIs within $0.30; seed-count convergence diagnostic shows `cam_multisignal` and ρ stable from 20 seeds (§9.2.2; caveat: N = 10 is vacuously stable and the path is non-monotone, §9.2 #17); macro-F1 (§8.9) provides per-class F1 beyond raw match accuracy; common random numbers (§8.9) confirm CRN changes profits by <$3 and does not affect conclusions; all p-values survive Bonferroni correction (§7.3). v1.3 extensions (§8.10–§8.17) add 8 ablations: adversarial mapping, channel-dependent reward, bandit baselines, class-conditional noise, convergence trend, perception dose-response, crisis bid policy, and sensing fidelity. v1.4 extensions (§8.18–§8.20) add online reward-feedback learning (UCB1 recovers 83.0% of the fixed-mapping value with zero labeled action data; F10), a mid-episode drift condition (post-drift recalibration is net-negative; multi-signal inference is drift-robust), and flat-taxonomy confusion quantification (intent-only 11.0% cross-axis confusion vs 1.0% multi-signal). The v1.5 extension (§8.21) adds the pre-registered LLM zero-shot classification baseline (API models at temperature 0: 79.9–83.0% accuracy; F11). Note: ρ(match rate, profit) is an environment-consistency check, not a dose-response law — the match bonus is a linear component of profit (§9.2 #11).
5. **Theoretical:** Grounded CAM in Endsley's SA model (Levels 1–3 situational awareness applied to marketing automation)
6. **Conceptual:** Created a four-layer framework for context-aware marketing agents

### 10.3 Future Work & Field-Validation Design
1. **Pre-specified field validation (priority):** two-arm experiment with a B2B partner (candidate: the proposed G6 collaboration): **Arm A** = CAM decisioning (multi-signal situation classifier → action mapper, exactly the `cam_multisignal_recalibrated` pipeline), **Arm B** = business-as-usual rule-based targeting. Primary endpoint: profit/conversion uplift per campaign; secondary: match-rate audit of agent classifications against human-coded situations. Design: ≥ 40 campaigns per arm over 8–12 weeks, analyzed with mixed-effects models (campaign as random effect) — the field analogue of CAM-Sim's paired-seed design.
2. **Learned bidding:** train the bid layer against the clearing mechanism (F8 shows this changes conclusions under concave returns); evaluate against situation_only as the null.
3. **Adversarial-mapping condition (✅ done, Section 8.11):** adversarial mapping (0.4% match, -$268.88 vs +$206.13, d = 29.28) proves the benchmark can falsify a wrong mapping.
4. **De-confound perception from bidding (✅ done, Section 8.15):** flat-bid dose-response (bid = 1.0, p ∈ {0.5,...,1.0}) shows monotone profit increase (+$128.79 → +$294.27, per-seed ρ = 0.998); perception→profit is not a bid-policy artifact.
5. **Common random numbers (✅ done, §8.9):** CRN (per-agent RNG, same contexts) changes profits by <$3 for all stochastic agents (baseline: +$0.81; noisy50: −$2.62; noisy80: +$0.86); all deterministic agents are unaffected. CRN does not change any conclusion.
6. **Macro-F1 and per-class recall (✅ done, §8.9):** macro-F1 is now reported for all classifier agents. 3-signal classifiers (0.92–1.00) substantially outperform 1-signal classifiers (0.46–0.73); `cam_inferred`'s low macro-F1 (0.4696) reflects its 4-of-6-class structural handicap.
7. **Adversarial & dynamic environments:** competitor adaptation, context drift, multi-period state carryover.
8. **Field studies:** deploy CAM with marketer-in-the-loop in production settings.
9. **B2B extension:** value-context Layer 0 (G6) — the value-opportunity-recognition construct (Böhm et al., 2020) operationalized as the sensing capability.
10. **Theory:** formal treatment of marketing situational awareness (Levels 1–3 as measurable perception-quality intervals).
11. **Matched-family ablation (✅ done, §8.6) and standard classifiers (✅ done, §8.8):** benchmarked nearest-centroid on intent-only vs nearest-centroid on 3 signals — the +$103.85 gap (p = 5.16e-46; d_z = 7.89 is t/√50 from a deterministic paired comparison, not a generalizable effect size) is signal-driven. The fitted 3-signal vs fitted 1-signal gap is +$103.10 (cam_multisignal_learned vs ncc_intent_only, both fitted). **Standard classifiers (LR, RF) on 1 and 3 signals confirm the multi-signal advantage is classifier-family-robust:** LR +$65.48 (p = 2.2e-40), RF +$75.98 (p = 3.2e-37). RF-3 achieves 100% match and slightly beats NCC (+$3.66, p = 1.0e-12), confirming the 3-signal structure is fully learnable and the NCC ceiling (98.9%) is a classifier-family limitation, not a signal limitation.
12. **Contextual-bandit baseline (✅ done, Section 8.12):** offline-trained ε-greedy (ε=0.1, +$169.91, -$36.22 vs cam_multisignal) and LinUCB-style linear profit model (+$91.42, -$114.71) both underperform; the reward landscape is nonlinear. Online adaptation: **partially resolved (Section 8.18):** ε-greedy Q and UCB1 with a reward-feedback loop recover 72.5–83.0% of the fixed-mapping value from observed profit alone (F10).
13. **Class-conditional label noise (✅ done, Section 8.13):** class-conditional noise (crisis↔decision, retention↔exploration) at 30% drops match to 89.2% and profit to +$172.27 (vs clean 98.7%/$205.39, -$33.11, p = 1.13e-34).
14. **Paired-difference bootstrap with B ≥ 2,000 (✅ done, §8.6):** bootstrapped the cam_multisignal − cam_inferred and cam_multisignal_learned − cam_learned contrasts directly (B = 2000); paired-diff CIs are within $0.30 of normal-approximation CIs.
15. **Account-level aggregation for B2B:** model 6–10 stakeholders per account with per-stakeholder situations (§9.2 #19).
16. **First-price auction mechanism:** replace the exogenous-clearing-price model with a first-price or quality-scored auction; test whether F3 survives under realistic bidding dynamics (§9.2 #20).
17. **Crisis action mapping (✅ done, Section 8.16):** pause (bid = 0) earns +$225.10 vs counter (2.0×) +$206.13 in the default environment; in crisis_heavy, pause +$243.79 vs counter +$141.93. The 2.0× multiplier overbids in low-reward crisis situations; pausing is consistently better.
18. **Sensing-layer fidelity (✅ done, Section 8.17):** replacing the decoy `quality_score` with the real `channel_quality` (sensing_fidelity = True) produces identical results for all four classifier agents, confirming the sensing layer is decorative.
19. **Majority-action baseline (✅ done, §8.6):** an always-EDUCATIONAL, flat-bid agent achieves 35.1% match and −$15.38 profit — better than the random-action baseline (21.6%, −$170.03) but still negative; however, this is the strongest context-blind policy *on the SEARCH channel only* — a context-blind EMAIL policy earns +$252 (§8.7).
20. **Channel-dependent reward (✅ done, Section 8.10, §8.7, §9.2 #25):** a channel-dependent reward condition (mismatch penalty 0.5) drops cb_edu_email_030 from +$252.41 to +$132.72 while cam_multisignal is nearly unchanged (+$206.13 → +$205.77); the arbitrage disappears. The default environment (channel-independent reward) remains the primary benchmark; the channel-dependent condition is a sensitivity check.
21. **Online reward-feedback learning (✅ done, Section 8.18):** ε-greedy Q and UCB1 agents over (estimated situation × action type) cells learn the mapping from observed profit alone within a 200-step episode: UCB1 recovers 83.0% of the baseline→cam_multisignal gap (default) / 81.3% (crisis_heavy) and beats the hand-tuned cam_inferred without labeled data (F10). Bottleneck: rare situations (≈10 episode visits). Open: joint (action × channel × bid) online learning; online bid-layer adaptation.
22. **Dynamic environments (partial ✅, Section 8.19; was §10.3 #7):** a mid-episode drift condition (default → crisis_heavy at scenario 100) shows F5 replicates within-episode and **F6's recalibration remedy reverses**: post-drift refit is net-negative over the episode (−$15.08, p = 1.56e-05) because it trades first-half performance for second-half recovery; multi-signal inference is drift-robust (+$102.82/+$71.25 across halves). Remaining: competitor adaptation, multi-period state carryover, drift-detection-triggered windowed refits.
23. **Flat-taxonomy confusion quantification (✅ done, Section 8.20):** the intent-only classifier's errors concentrate in the cross-axis pairs the taxonomy predicts (crisis→decision 49.9%, opportunity→consideration 52.0%, retention→exploration 53.7%; 11.0% cross-axis overall) at −$1.63 to −$2.79 per confused scenario; multi-signal cuts cross-axis confusion to 1.0%. The orthogonal (stage × condition) environment remains future work.
24. **LLM zero-shot classifier baseline (✅ done — empirically complete, Section 8.21, v1.5 + Section 8.22, v1.6; executed 2026-11-02):** the pre-registered protocol was implemented verbatim (`cam_sim_v15_llm.py`): textualized observations (incl. the decoy signal), temperature-0 LLM classification against qualitative definitions only, fixed action/channel/bid mapper, paired evaluation on 
