46:We present **CAM-Sim**, a reproducible paired-seed benchmark for situational-awareness ablations in marketing agents: 50 seeds × 200 scenarios, eleven agents on a ladder from context-blind baseline through signal-based classifiers to a labeled oracle. Multi-signal situation classification raises match from 87.5% to 98.7% and profit from +$155 to +$205 (+$50, p = 8.5e-32, d_z = 3.96). A matched-family ablation (same NCC family, 1 vs 3 signals) isolates the gain as signal-driven (+$103.85 (hand-set 3-signal vs fitted 1-signal), p = 5.16e-46; +$103.10 (fitted 3-signal vs fitted 1-signal), p = 1.60e-45). Standard classifier baselines (logistic regression, random forest) on 1 and 3 signals confirm the multi-signal advantage is classifier-family-robust (LR +$65.48, p = 2.2e-40; RF +$75.98, p = 3.2e-37; RF-3 achieves 100% match, slightly beating NCC). A context-blind policy search reveals a **channel-cost arbitrage**: EDUCATIONAL on EMAIL (cost $0.05) at bid 0.3 earns +$252, beating every deployable context-aware agent in 7 of 9 environments — the reward is channel-independent, so the cheapest channel dominates (§8.7). The advantage replicates across 9 environments (per-seed ρ ≥ 0.93), survives 30% label noise and budget pacing, and is checked by paired-difference bootstrap (B = 2000), macro-F1 (per-class), and common random numbers. We contribute a reproducible benchmark, environment-consistency evidence, and a pre-specified field-validation design. Eight v1.3 extensions (adversarial mapping, channel-dependent reward, bandit baselines, class-conditional noise, convergence trend, perception dose-response, crisis bid policy, sensing fidelity) close the remaining simulatable threats to validity. Three v1.4 extensions push further: online reward-feedback learners recover up to 83% of the fixed-mapping value from observed profit alone (§8.18); recalibrating to a mid-episode distribution shift is net-negative (−$15.08, p = 1.6e-05), refining the recalibration remedy (§8.19); and the flat taxonomy's predicted confusions are quantified (11.0% cross-axis confusion for intent-only vs 1.0% multi-signal, §8.20). A v1.5 extension executes the pre-registered LLM baseline: zero-shot classifiers (temperature 0, qualitative definitions only; five models from LAN-local to 128B hosted) reach 74–83% raw accuracy — profit-competitive with the hand-tuned classifier but below it on balanced accuracy (macro-recall 50–61% vs 65.9%), losing to supervised classifiers, with emergent ≤4-of-6 blind spots (§8.21); the v1.6 extension shows the blindness is prior-driven (0/210 counterfactual probes ever elicit crisis/retention), prompt remediation restores recall only at a profit/accuracy cost (hints: Mistral +$7.49, glm -$57.75; few-shot: both -$69 to -$88), and end-to-end LLM action selection exceeds cam_multisignal without any fitted component (+$250–273 vs +$206–220).
717:We presented **CAM-Sim** — a reproducible paired-seed benchmark for situational-awareness ablations in marketing agents. In 50 seeds × 200 scenarios (10,000 evaluations per agent), multi-signal situation classification (intent + competitive density + channel quality) raises match from 87.5% (learned single-signal) to 98.7% (learned multi-signal) and profit from +$155 to +$205 (+$50, p = 8.5e-32, d_z = 3.96); against a hand-tuned threshold classifier, the gain is +$102 (p = 5.4e-46). A matched-family ablation (same NCC family, 1 vs 3 signals) isolates the gain as signal-driven (+$103.85 (hand-set 3-signal vs fitted 1-signal), p = 5.16e-46; +$103.10 (fitted 3-signal vs fitted 1-signal), p = 1.60e-45); a majority-action floor (35.1%, −$15.38) retires the random-action baseline (strongest on the SEARCH channel). A context-blind policy search reveals a **channel-cost arbitrage**: EDUCATIONAL on EMAIL (cost $0.05) at bid 0.3 earns +$252, beating every deployable context-aware agent in 7 of 9 environments (§8.7) — the environment's reward is channel-independent, so the cheapest channel dominates; the multi-signal gain is measured *within* the context-aware ladder, not against all context-free policies. The advantage replicates across 9 environments (per-seed ρ ≥ 0.93), survives 30% label noise and budget pacing, and is checked by paired-difference bootstrap (B = 2000, CIs within $0.30), macro-F1 (per-class), and common random numbers (CRN changes profits by <$3). Standard classifier baselines (LR, RF) on 1 and 3 signals confirm the multi-signal gain is classifier-family-robust (LR +$65.48, RF +$75.98; RF-3 achieves 100% match). Three v1.4 extensions close further gaps: online reward-feedback learners recover up to 83.0% of the fixed-mapping value without labeled action data (§8.18); under mid-episode distribution drift, recalibrating to the post-shift distribution is net-negative (−$15.08, p = 1.6e-05) while multi-signal inference is drift-robust (§8.19); and the flat taxonomy's forced confusions are quantified at −$1.63 to −$2.79 per confused scenario, concentrated exactly in the predicted cross-axis pairs (§8.20). The v1.5 extension executes the pre-registered LLM baseline: zero-shot classifiers reach 74–83% raw accuracy — profit-competitive with the hand-tuned classifier but below it on balanced accuracy (macro-recall 50–61% vs 65.9%), all losing to supervised classifiers, with emergent ≤4-of-6 blind spots on the overlap classes (§8.21, F11); the v1.6 extension shows the blindness is prior-driven (0/210 counterfactual probes ever elicit crisis/retention), prompt remediation restores recall only at a profit/accuracy cost (hints: Mistral +$7.49, glm -$57.75; few-shot: both -$69 to -$88), and end-to-end LLM action selection exceeds cam_multisignal without any fitted component (+$250–273 vs +$206–220). All profits are scaled-reward proxies (§9.2 #12). The central practical finding: invest first in **multi-signal situation classification**, then in **mechanism-calibrated bidding** — but channel-cost structure can dominate signal quality (§8.7); unvalidated bid heuristics are worse than not bidding at all.
722:3. **Ablation (§8.6, §8.7):** Matched-family ablation (same NCC family, 1 vs 3 signals) isolates the multi-signal gain as signal-driven, not classifier-capacity-driven (+$103.85 (hand-set 3-signal vs fitted 1-signal), p = 5.16e-46, d_z = 7.89; +$103.10 (fitted 3-signal vs fitted 1-signal), p = 1.60e-45; d_z is t/√50 from a deterministic paired comparison — it measures simulation reproducibility, not a generalizable effect size); majority-action floor (35.1%, −$15.38) retires the random-action baseline (strongest on the SEARCH channel); **context-blind policy search (§8.7)** reveals a channel-cost arbitrage — EDUCATIONAL|EMAIL|bid 0.3 earns +$252.41, beating every deployable context-aware agent in 7 of 9 environments — the paper claims multi-signal value *within* the context-aware ladder, not dominance over all context-free policies; paired-difference bootstrap (B = 2000) confirms normal-approximation CIs within $0.30.
738:11. **Matched-family ablation (✅ done, §8.6) and standard classifiers (✅ done, §8.8):** benchmarked nearest-centroid on intent-only vs nearest-centroid on 3 signals — the +$103.85 gap (p = 5.16e-46; d_z = 7.89 is t/√50 from a deterministic paired comparison, not a generalizable effect size) is signal-driven. The fitted 3-signal vs fitted 1-signal gap is +$103.10 (cam_multisignal_learned vs ncc_intent_only, both fitted). **Standard classifiers (LR, RF) on 1 and 3 signals confirm the multi-signal advantage is classifier-family-robust:** LR +$65.48 (p = 2.2e-40), RF +$75.98 (p = 3.2e-37). RF-3 achieves 100% match and slightly beats NCC (+$3.66, p = 1.0e-12), confirming the 3-signal structure is fully learnable and the NCC ceiling (98.9%) is a classifier-family limitation, not a signal limitation.
=== learned-vs-learned / +50.01 occurrences md ===
344:**F9 (multi-signal awareness — the framework's core claim, confirmed):** Adding competitive density and channel quality to the intent-only signal raises match and profit. **The fair comparison is learned-vs-learned:** `cam_multisignal_learned` (98.7%, +$205.39) vs `cam_learned` (87.5%, +$155.38) — a +$50.01 gain (p = 8.5e-32, d_z = 3.96, 95% CI [+$46.5, +$53.5]), confirming that multi-signal structure is recoverable from data alone. **Against the hand-tuned threshold classifier** (`cam_inferred`, 75.7%, 4-of-6 classes, +$104.26), the gain is larger: +$101.87 (p = 5.4e-46, d_z = 7.88) — within $3.87 of the oracle (+$210.00). The advantage is **universal on the handicapped comparator**: `cam_multisignal` beats `cam_inferred` in all 9 environments (§8.4), with the largest swings exactly where intent-only classification collapses — `crisis_heavy` (−$66.8 vs +$141.9, a +$208.7 swing) and `retention_heavy` (−$15.5 vs +$237.2, +$252.7). The learned-vs-learned comparison is not reported per-environment (§10.3 #11). Mechanism: intent alone conflates crisis↔decision (both high intent) and retention↔exploration (both low intent); competitive density and channel quality resolve both ambiguities. This is the operational validation of CAM Layer 1 (*sense multi-modal context signals*): multi-signal awareness is not a marginal improvement but the difference between sub-oracle and near-oracle performance.
346:**Interpretation:** **Multi-signal situation classification is the first investment; bid modulation is a force multiplier that is only as good as its calibration.** Single-signal intent inference leaves roughly a quarter of the deployable value on the table (learned-vs-learned: +$155.38 vs +$205.39, a 24% gap), or roughly half against the handicapped comparator (+$104.26 vs +$206.13), and naive context-inflated bidding — the natural heuristic a practitioner would deploy — is *worse than doing nothing at the bid layer*. The CAM value proposition: invest first in **multi-signal situation classification** (intent + competitive density + channel quality), then in mechanism-calibrated bidding, and never in unvalidated bid heuristics.
426:**Matched-family ablation.** A nearest-centroid classifier on intent-only (`ncc_intent_only`, same NCC family as `cam_multisignal`, 1 signal instead of 3) achieves 72.7% match and +$102.28 profit. The 3-signal NCC (`cam_multisignal`, 98.9% match, +$206.13) outperforms it by +$103.85 (p = 5.16e-46, paired-diff bootstrap CI [+$100.13, +$107.55]). **Disclosure:** `cam_multisignal` uses hand-set generative centroids (the true means), while `ncc_intent_only` uses centroids fitted from a 2,000-sample calibration (seed 999999); the 3-signal arm is therefore an upper bound on the fitted NCC estimator, not a lower bound. The learned-vs-learned comparison (`cam_multisignal_learned` vs `cam_learned`, both fitted) gives +$50.01 (p = 8.5e-32, d_z = 3.96) — the conservative learned-vs-learned estimate. The +$103.85 gap is the family-matched maximum (hand-set 3-signal vs fitted 1-signal); the learned-vs-learned gap (+$50.01) is the conservative estimate. Both are signal-driven, not classifier-capacity-driven (same NCC family, same bid policy, same action mapping; differs in signal count and fitting method).
433:| cam_multisignal_learned − cam_learned (learned-vs-learned) | +$50.01 | [+$46.62, +$53.69] | [+$46.42, +$53.60] |
451:The best context-blind policy (EDUCATIONAL|EMAIL|bid 0.3, +$252.41) beats every deployable context-aware agent, including `cam_multisignal` (+$206.13), `cam_multisignal_learned` (+$205.39), and `cam_learned` (+$155.38), in 7 of 9 environments. This is a **benchmark-construction limitation** (§9.2 #25): the environment's reward function does not penalize wrong-channel assignments, so the cheapest channel dominates. The multi-signal classification gain (87.5% → 98.7%, +$50 learned-vs-learned) is measured *within* the context-aware ladder; the channel-cost arbitrage shows that a context-blind policy on a cheap channel can outperform a context-aware policy on an expensive channel. The paper's contribution is the *relative* value of multi-signal classification (F9, §8.6), not a claim that context-aware agents beat all context-free policies. **Resolved (Section 8.10):** A channel-dependent reward condition (mismatch penalty 0.5) drops cb_edu_email_030 from +$252.41 to +$132.72 while cam_multisignal is nearly unchanged (+$206.13 -> +$205.77); the arbitrage disappears.
478:**Interpretation:** The multi-signal classification gain is robust across three classifier families (NCC, LR, RF). The gain magnitude varies by family (NCC: +$103.85 hand-set / +$50.01 learned; LR: +$65.48; RF: +$75.98), but the direction is consistent: 3 signals > 1 signal. RF-3's 100% match confirms the clusters are well-separated; the residual headroom above NCC (98.9%) is classifier capacity, not signal structure. **Resolved (Section 8.12):** Offline-trained epsilon-greedy (epsilon=0.1, +$169.91) and LinUCB-style linear profit model (+$91.42) both underperform cam_multisignal (+$206.13); the reward landscape is nonlinear.
629:**Honest framing of the oracle:** The oracle is an upper bound that validates environment consistency. The scientifically meaningful agents are `cam_multisignal` (three-signal classifier, 98.9% match, within $4 of oracle — but the $3.87 gap is itself significant: p = 1.3e-13, d_z = 1.43), `cam_multisignal_learned` (98.7%, the evidence that the structure is recoverable from data alone), `cam_inferred` (single-signal, 75.7% match, 4-of-6 classes), and the noisy agents (graded perception). The hand-set `cam_multisignal` uses the true generative centroids; its 98.9% match is the **nearest-centroid lower bound, not the Bayes rate** — a prior-weighted MAP classifier on the same features achieves ≈99.2%. The learned variant (98.7%) is the real empirical result. The **fair single-signal comparator is `cam_learned`** (87.5%, +$155.38), not the handicapped `cam_inferred` (75.7%, +$104.26): the learned-vs-learned gain is +$50.01 (p = 8.5e-32, d_z = 3.96), while the handicapped-vs-multisignal gain is +$101.87. The environment-consistency across these — not oracle-vs-baseline — is the paper's core empirical claim.
644:13. **Missing comparators:** **Resolved (§8.8):** Logistic Regression (LR) and Random Forest (RF) baselines are now benchmarked on 1-signal and 3-signal inputs, trained on the same 2000-sample calibration set as the NCC learners. The multi-signal advantage holds across all three classifier families: NCC +$103.85 (hand-set) / +$50.01 (learned), LR +$65.48 (p = 2.2e-40), RF +$75.98 (p = 3.2e-37). NCC outperforms LR on the same signals (−$21.69) because NCC is Bayes-optimal for the Gaussian generative model; RF slightly beats NCC (+$3.66, 100% match) because its nonlinear boundaries learn the exact cluster edges. **Resolved (Section 8.12):** Offline-trained epsilon-greedy (epsilon=0.1, +$169.91, -$36.22 vs cam_multisignal) and LinUCB-style linear profit model (+$91.42, -$114.71) both underperform cam_multisignal (+$206.13); the reward landscape is nonlinear. Online adaptation: **partially resolved (§8.18):** online agents with a reward-feedback loop (ε-greedy Q, UCB1) learn the action mapping from observed profit alone within the episode, recovering 72.5–83.0% of the fixed-mapping value; UCB1 (+$142.13) beats the hand-tuned `cam_inferred` (+$104.26) without any labeled data. Full convergence needs more than a 200-step window (rare-situation Q-cells stay under-sampled); online adaptation of the *bid* layer remains future work.
645:14. **Classifier family = generative family:** The nearest-centroid classifier is Bayes-optimal only in the isotropic/equal-prior special case; the generator uses unequal priors and non-isotropic features (intent SD 0.1, competition/quality SD 0.15), so 98.9% is a lower bound, not the Bayes rate (a prior-weighted MAP classifier achieves ≈99.2%). `cam_multisignal` (hand-set centroids = true means) therefore achieves the generator's lower bound, not an empirical classifier result. `cam_multisignal_learned` (98.7%) confirms the estimator is unbiased but cannot demonstrate recovery under realistic conditions (shifted, noisy, or partly missing signals). The headline +$101.87 gain is measured against a handicapped comparator (`cam_inferred`, 4-of-6 classes); the learned-vs-learned gain is +$50.01 (§9.1, F9).
